receipt-verifier v1.0.3 523e0a2b822088990f500b9eea3868309439c53c
# ── ONE RELEASED TAG, AND IT IS NOW TRUE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Line 1 used to be a bare commit, and the note under it said the files "keep their own earlier
# revisions on purpose — a single source-commit line would be tidier and untrue". That was honest
# about a mixed set. MEASURED across the WHOLE directory before this changed: every file here is
# byte-identical to receipt-verifier v1.0.0, so the single line is no longer tidier-and-untrue —
# it is simply true, and it is a name a stranger can resolve. A working tree or a bare commit in a
# repo nobody else has is not a provenance.
#
# Three files were stale and were re-copied FROM THE TAG (not from a local HEAD):
#   verified-execution-binding.js  e48ea29b -> 9d0259e9   (closed profiles, receipt verification)
#   verify.js                      363e52c7 -> 348d3f85   (CLI docs only: 0 non-comment lines)
#   verify-grant.js                127b53de -> 4a0ad5bd   (the reserved inert v2 fields)
# verify-attest.js already matched (035105de) and was not touched.
#
# demo/test/vendor-core.test.js compares these bytes against `git show v1.0.0:<file>` when
# receipt-verifier is beside this repo, so the tag is checked and not merely asserted.
#
#
# ── AND THE TAG IS SIGNED, WHICH IS THE PART A STRANGER CAN CHECK ───────────────────────────
#
# v1.0.0 was an unsigned tag: a name anyone can check out, and also a name anyone with push access
# can move. v1.0.1 is annotated and SSH-signed, so the pin now resolves to an identity and not
# only to bytes.
#
#   tag object   bb5753681275920e7e23351c6b41632957e6238f
#   peeled commit 523e0a2b822088990f500b9eea3868309439c53c
#   signer       zsobpeter@gmail.com
#   fingerprint  SHA256:7yRXTm9zKGicfFpzL+7lpwFoPaoSwxAJlabB3jwxw2Y
#
# Verify it yourself, in a checkout of receipt-verifier:
#
#   git tag -v v1.0.3
#   -> Good "git" signature for zsobpeter@gmail.com with ED25519 key SHA256:7yRXTm9zKGicfFpzL+7lpwFoPaoSwxAJlabB3jwxw2Y
#
# WHY v1.0.3 AND NOT v1.0.2: the CORE IS NOT byte-identical. verify-grant.js admits
# applied_policy_hash on V2_RESERVED_INERT (1942 verifier-admits; fail-closed otherwise
# unchanged) and verify.js moved with it. This re-pin copies those bytes from the signed
# tag, not from a working tree.
#
# The peeled commit is 523e0a2 — the K-phase-1 inert-admit commit the tag names.
